WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.397
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:08.897
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.197 --> 00:00:17.852
Unit forty-four: Rhetoric and Public Speaking. In this unit, you will analyse and use persuasive speaking techniques.

4
00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:19.874
ethos.

5
00:00:20.574 --> 00:00:24.385
ethos means persuasion based on the speaker's credibility.

6
00:00:24.685 --> 00:00:28.113
A doctor speaking about health has strong ethos.

7
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:31.221
Your turn. Say: ethos.

8
00:00:33.921 --> 00:00:34.922
ethos.

9
00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:36.836
pathos.

10
00:00:37.536 --> 00:00:39.942
pathos means persuasion through emotion.

11
00:00:40.242 --> 00:00:43.298
The charity advert relied heavily on pathos.

12
00:00:43.998 --> 00:00:46.825
Your turn. Say: pathos.

13
00:00:49.525 --> 00:00:50.630
pathos.

14
00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:52.560
logos.

15
00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.571
logos means persuasion through logic and evidence.

16
00:00:56.871 --> 00:01:00.972
Her argument was full of logos: data, facts and clear reasoning.

17
00:01:01.672 --> 00:01:04.452
Your turn. Say: logos.

18
00:01:07.152 --> 00:01:07.991
logos.

19
00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:10.402
rhetorical question.

20
00:01:11.102 --> 00:01:15.505
rhetorical question means a question asked for effect, not for an answer.

21
00:01:15.805 --> 00:01:19.282
'Who doesn't want a better future?' is a rhetorical question.

22
00:01:19.982 --> 00:01:23.296
Your turn. Say: rhetorical question.

23
00:01:25.996 --> 00:01:27.310
rhetorical question.

24
00:01:28.317 --> 00:01:29.283
tricolon.

25
00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:33.828
tricolon means a series of three parallel words or phrases.

26
00:01:34.128 --> 00:01:39.042
'Government of the people, by the people, for the people' is a famous tricolon.

27
00:01:39.742 --> 00:01:42.661
Your turn. Say: tricolon.

28
00:01:45.361 --> 00:01:46.293
tricolon.

29
00:01:47.283 --> 00:01:48.145
anaphora.

30
00:01:48.845 --> 00:01:53.051
anaphora means repeating words at the start of successive sentences.

31
00:01:53.351 --> 00:01:57.092
'We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight...' uses anaphora.

32
00:01:57.792 --> 00:02:00.572
Your turn. Say: anaphora.

33
00:02:03.272 --> 00:02:04.331
anaphora.

34
00:02:05.317 --> 00:02:06.840
persuasive technique.

35
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:12.013
persuasive technique means a method used to convince an audience.

36
00:02:12.313 --> 00:02:16.309
Repetition is a simple but effective persuasive technique.

37
00:02:17.009 --> 00:02:20.474
Your turn. Say: persuasive technique.

38
00:02:23.174 --> 00:02:24.593
persuasive technique.

39
00:02:25.583 --> 00:02:27.258
audience analysis.

40
00:02:27.958 --> 00:02:32.651
audience analysis means studying who your listeners are before you speak.

41
00:02:32.951 --> 00:02:36.634
Good audience analysis shapes the tone of a speech.

42
00:02:37.334 --> 00:02:40.543
Your turn. Say: audience analysis.

43
00:02:43.243 --> 00:02:45.022
audience analysis.

44
00:02:46.017 --> 00:02:48.179
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:48.479 --> 00:02:53.186
Ms. Sarah, I have to give a speech next week. What makes a speech persuasive?

46
00:02:54.483 --> 00:02:58.459
What matters first is audience analysis. Who will be listening?

47
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.796
Students and some lecturers.

48
00:03:03.083 --> 00:03:07.324
Then it's credibility, or ethos, that you'll need to establish early.

49
00:03:08.617 --> 00:03:09.595
And emotion?

50
00:03:10.883 --> 00:03:17.434
Pathos helps, but what really convinces academics is logos: clear evidence.

51
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.655
Should I use techniques like rhetorical questions?

52
00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:28.540
Yes, sparingly. What audiences remember most is a powerful tricolon at the end.

53
00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:33.231
Grammar focus: Cleft sentences (mastery).

54
00:03:33.531 --> 00:03:36.819
Cleft sentences are powerful tools in public speaking.

55
00:03:37.119 --> 00:03:42.674
What matters first is audience analysis. It's credibility that you'll need to establish.

56
00:03:42.974 --> 00:03:46.704
What audiences remember most is a powerful ending.

57
00:03:47.004 --> 00:03:51.987
Speakers use cleft sentences to signal their most important points to listeners.

58
00:03:52.983 --> 00:03:56.019
Now read and listen. The Power of Words.

59
00:03:56.319 --> 00:04:02.022
More than two thousand years ago, Aristotle identified three ways to persuade an audience.

60
00:04:03.317 --> 00:04:09.415
Ethos relies on the speaker's credibility, pathos on emotion, and logos on logic.

61
00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:15.921
Great speeches combine all three, adapting them through careful audience analysis.

62
00:04:17.217 --> 00:04:21.202
Speakers also use rhetorical techniques to make their messages memorable.

63
00:04:22.517 --> 00:04:27.546
A rhetorical question invites the audience to think, while a tricolon creates rhythm.

64
00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:34.925
Anaphora, the repetition of words at the start of sentences, builds emotional intensity.

65
00:04:36.217 --> 00:04:41.571
Martin Luther King's famous speech used anaphora when he repeated 'I have a dream'.

66
00:04:42.883 --> 00:04:47.797
It is not only what we say, but how we say it, that moves people to act.

67
00:04:49.083 --> 00:04:51.829
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:52.129 --> 00:04:55.185
Persuasion through logic and evidence is blank.

69
00:04:55.485 --> 00:04:59.370
A, ethos. B, pathos. C, logos.

70
00:05:03.383 --> 00:05:05.708
The answer is C: logos.

71
00:05:07.217 --> 00:05:09.077
blank you need is confidence.

72
00:05:09.377 --> 00:05:12.322
A, That. B, What. C, It.

73
00:05:16.317 --> 00:05:18.386
The answer is B: What.

74
00:05:19.883 --> 00:05:23.148
It was his final sentence blank everyone remembered.

75
00:05:23.448 --> 00:05:26.695
A, that. B, what. C, which it.

76
00:05:30.683 --> 00:05:32.369
The answer is A: that.

77
00:05:33.883 --> 00:05:37.264
Repeating words at the start of successive sentences is blank.

78
00:05:37.564 --> 00:05:42.901
A, tricolon. B, anaphora. C, audience analysis.

79
00:05:46.917 --> 00:05:49.253
The answer is B: anaphora.

80
00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.212
According to the text, which speech used anaphora?

81
00:05:54.512 --> 00:06:01.184
A, Martin Luther King's 'I have a dream'. B, Aristotle's lecture. C, a charity advert.

82
00:06:05.183 --> 00:06:09.075
The answer is A: Martin Luther King's 'I have a dream'.

83
00:06:10.583 --> 00:06:17.311
Now it's your turn. Part one: Prepare the opening of a short speech about the importance of learning English.

84
00:06:22.611 --> 00:06:29.185
Part two: Use ethos, pathos and logos, and include a tricolon or rhetorical question.

85
00:06:35.485 --> 00:06:49.764
As a student who once struggled with English, I know how it feels. Who doesn't want more opportunities? English opens doors to education, to employment, and to the world. What it gives us, above all, is a voice.

86
00:06:50.064 --> 00:06:52.435
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:58.494
Great work today! You learned eight new words and cleft sentences (mastery).

88
00:06:58.794 --> 00:07:02.851
Today's value is self-confidence. Believe in yourself.

89
00:07:03.151 --> 00:07:06.070
See you in Unit forty-five. Goodbye!

90
00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.487
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

